 {"id": "PERF-011", "title": "P2 as-of recall: 177ms on scratch ns but 7.2s p50 warm on prod default ns (245k rows)", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "source": null, "reasoning": "Real-use as-of recall (flagship time-travel surface, first dogfood exercise) measured: HTTP GET /api/memories?as_of=... on scratch namespace (2 memories, 2 commits) avg 177ms max 737ms (n=20 warm, curl time_total); same endpoint on prod daemon :3000 default namespace (245k rows) p50 7248ms p100 8158ms warm (n=15, incl 5-run 4.3-4.6s block then 7-8s block). Command: curl -H 'x-api-key: ...' 'http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/memories?prefix=/&namespace=default&as_of=2026-10-01T00:00:00Z&limit=10'. Scaling from 177ms to 7.2s over 5 orders of magnitude of data suggests per-partition git show fan-out or full-scan before limit. Hot path: src/git/asof.ts readRowsAsOf -> per-partition git show; src/http/routes/memories.ts:316. Fix direction: profile with node --cpu-prof on the prod-size namespace; cache per-partition as-of reads; consider rev-list --before resolution cache. Not user-blocking for small namespaces; 7s on the flagship prod ns is user-noticeable.", "evidence": null, "created_at": "2026-10-03T13:07:43.206379+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T13:07:43.206379+00:00", "detail": {"fingerprint": "a4e78e76ff453b32ba3c9f1a28f7d5de37c36d2df95db2af4ea1cc5e1f0f6742", "evidence": [{"run_id": "dogfood-2026-10-03", "ts": "2026-10-03T13:07:43.206379+00:00"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "completed_at": null, "commit_hash": null, "attempts": 0, "files_changed": [], "guard_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null, "capability_tags": ["perf", "asof"]}
 {"id": "DF-1003-03", "title": "P3 'default' namespace silently auto-creates outside explicit namespace create; fresh-box quickstart as-of then 404s", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 3, "source": null, "reasoning": "On the bunker fresh box (HEAD f463832), POSTing memories WITHOUT first creating a namespace wrote into 'default' (auto-created, git history landed: commit aa10bb7 contains the rows) while the client believed ns=df-asof existed from an earlier partial step; later ?namespace=df-asof recall returned 'Namespace does not exist' (ApiError memories.ts:69) and an as-of query on an empty ns dir without git history returned the good error 'not a git repository - as-of recall requires git history'. The auto-create WARN is logged server-side only - the 201 response never tells the caller the write went to a different namespace than intended. Fix direction: echo the effective namespace in the POST /api/memories response body (or reject writes to a namespace the request did not name). Discovered via arm3/arm4 of the 2026-10-03 as-of dogfood; see docs/dogfood/2026-10-03-asof-integration.md.", "evidence": null, "created_at": "2026-10-03T13:07:58.010748+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T13:07:58.010748+00:00", "detail": {"fingerprint": "dfb87e0839a75b18ac6c990997b6630febe8c03968d8fd3c9d6326cc6a2158da", "evidence": [{"run_id": "dogfood-2026-10-03", "ts": "2026-10-03T13:07:58.010748+00:00"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "completed_at": null, "commit_hash": null, "attempts": 0, "files_changed": [], "guard_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null, "capability_tags": ["namespaces", "ux"]}
 {"id": "S3-GIT-007", "title": "P1 \u2014 S3 BACKUP IS DOWN (3+ days): `duckbrain.js s3 sync all push` hangs in epoll and never completes, so every run is killed at the 7200s timeout and retried \u2014 a 2h15m hang\u2192kill\u2192retry loop. Last successful S3 run: 2026-09-30T12:46:44Z. S3-GIT-006 closed as complete but only fixed the fd-inheritance half; the stall it was filed for is still live, in the native sync layer.", "priority": "P1", "status": "complete", "created_at": "2026-10-03T17:46:44.404386+00:00", "created_by": null, "complexity": 3, "detail": {"fingerprint": "ce3fc5d08246dc2d1a7810ba1e2fafec74e9d1c6fcad67657410f8c1034b2bbc"}, "reasoning": "Found while answering a direct question about the S3 native tier. This is a FALSE-CLOSE finding: the row reads complete and the fix genuinely landed, but the operational symptom it was filed for is still happening. \n\nEVIDENCE THE BACKUP IS DEAD (measured, 2026-10-03): (1) cron job 'DuckBrain S3 unified backup (15min)' \u2014 schedule */15, enabled=true, no_agent=true (silent watchdog), last_status=error, last_error=\"Script timed out after 7200s: /home/kara/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-unified.sh\", last_run_at=2026-10-03T12:30:41, next_run_at=2026-10-03T13:00:00. (2) The backup log ~/.hermes/backups/duckbrain-s3daily.log has NOT ADVANCED since 2026-09-30T12:46:44Z \u2014 3.2 days. 354 successful runs are recorded historically; the last is 'OK: pushed=103 skipped=365 failed=0 forced=6 remote_repos=495 duration_s=949'. (3) Cadence markers are FROZEN at the same moment: s3-git-pass.last and s3-git-push.last both mtime 2026-09-30T07:46, s3-weekly-archive.last frozen 2026-09-24. So the 24h git layer and the 7d archive layer have not advanced either. \n\nTHE ACTUAL STALL (process tree captured live): pid 270287 bash duckbrain-s3-unified.sh [state do_wait, holding fd 9 -> duckbrain-s3-unified.lock] -> 270291 bash duckbrain-s3-native-sync.sh [state anon_pipe_read] -> 270310 bash duckbrain-s3-native-sync.sh [do_wait] -> 270311 node bin/duckbrain.js s3 sync all push [state ep_poll]. ep_poll means the deepest process is blocked waiting on network I/O. It sits there forever; nothing bounds this call. \n\nTHE LOOP IS VISIBLE IN ~/.hermes/backups/duckbrain-s3-native.log: 'STALE LOCK cleared pid=X age=~7500-8400s' every ~2h15m, with an intervening 'FAIL rc=143' (SIGTERM from the 7200s timeout). Reproduced entries: 2026-10-02-21:15:58, 2026-10-02-23:30:57, 2026-10-03-01:45:10, 2026-10-03-04:00:48, 2026-10-03-06:15:58, 2026-10-03-08:45:18, 2026-10-03-11:00:12 (with 'FAIL rc=143 2026-10-03-13:14:47'), 2026-10-03-15:30:41, 2026-10-03-17:45:04. So each run acquires the lock, hangs ~2h, is killed, and the next run clears the stale lock and repeats \u2014 indefinitely. \n\nWHY S3-GIT-006's CLOSE IS PARTIAL, NOT WRONG: the fix (c504336) is real and IS live. It bounded the S3 client request timeout (15s reject) and closed the inherited flock fd, and its own hermetic harness passes on the deployed script right now: `bash scripts/s3/test-fd-inheritance.sh` -> 'fd-inheritance harness: 14 passed, 0 failed', including 'arm5: the wrapper's running child (daily layer) has NO lockfile fd (S3-GIT-006 fix live)' and 'the lock is FREE again after the wrapper exited (no leak, no inherited copy anywhere)'. The repo copy and the deployed ~/.hermes/scripts copy are byte-identical (diff -q clean). BUT the row's own acceptance was 'the run terminates', and it does not: the hang moved to `duckbrain.js s3 sync all push`, which is not covered by the 15s client bound. The fd half is fixed; the stall half is open. \n\nWHY NOTHING SURFACED IT: the job is no_agent=true, i.e. a silent watchdog that is supposed to speak only on failure \u2014 and it IS failing, every 2h15m, with nothing reaching a human. Duration was already creeping toward the limit before the break: the last eight good runs were 716, 576, 615, 562, 828, 758, 1177, 949 seconds against a growing workload (remote_repos 260 -> 495). \n\nBLAST RADIUS: the NATIVE data-delta layer is the one that is stuck, so namespace DATA written since 2026-09-30 is not reaching S3; only local disk and git have it. The git-history and weekly-archive layers are also frozen (markers above). This is the nuke-readiness path \u2014 the reason the S3 tier exists \u2014 so the exposure is 'we would lose everything since 09-30 if this host died'. \n\nFIX DIRECTION: (a) bound the WHOLE push call, not just the client \u2014 wrap `s3 sync all push` in the native layer with a hard per-run deadline (and per-namespace where possible) so one unresponsive host cannot pin the entire 495-repo pass; (b) make the hang LOUD \u2014 the watchdog currently swallows a 3-day outage; emit an alert after N consecutive timeouts; (c) identify WHICH namespace/repo blocks in epoll (the log names the pid, not the repo) so the bad actor can be quarantined the way S3-GIT-002 already does for bundle-collision namespaces. \n\nACCEPTANCE: `s3 sync all push` cannot exceed a bounded wall time; a single unresponsive namespace cannot pin the pass; a full native pass completes and advances s3-git-pass.last/s3-git-push.last past 2026-09-30; and 3 consecutive timeout failures raise a visible alert instead of silence. \n\nEVIDENCE LEVEL: measured live (cron state, log mtimes, marker mtimes, live /proc process tree and fds, harness run). No S3 traffic was spent to prove this \u2014 the stall is observable locally. ", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T17:46:44.404386+00:00", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-10-04T11:10:00Z", "commit_hash": "aa9bb79", "worker_summary": "Per-ns deadline + wrapper timeout; sync-s3git007.test.ts 4/4; verdict b0c1da24 PASS", "files_changed": ["src/s3/sync.ts", "scripts/s3/duckbrain-s3-native-sync.sh", "src/s3/sync-s3git007.test.ts"]}
-{"id": "S3-ALERT-001", "title": "P2 — the S3 backup can fail for DAYS with no alert: nothing watches for consecutive failures", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "S3-GIT-007's outage was invisible for 3+ days. The backup itself is a silent watchdog and there is no layer above it that notices repeated failure.\n\nEvidence (live, 2026-10-03):\n  * cron job 1229f45f3ea4 'DuckBrain S3 unified backup (15min)', schedule */15, enabled=true, last_status=error, last_error='Script timed out after 7200s: /home/kara/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-unified.sh'.\n  * The job is no_agent (script-only), so a failure produces no agent turn and no message. Nothing surfaced the outage; it was found only because the owner happened to ask about the S3 tier.\n  * Last SUCCESSFUL run 2026-09-30T12:46:44Z; cadence markers s3-git-pass.last and s3-git-push.last both frozen at that instant; 354 successful runs before it.\n  * The wrapper has no failure-escalation path: grep -i 'consecutive|alert|notify' against ~/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-unified.sh returns nothing.\n  * Observed failure loop: native-sync log shows 'STALE LOCK cleared pid=X age=~7500-8400s' every ~2h15m with a 'FAIL rc=143' (the 7200s SIGTERM) between, from 10-02 21:15 through 10-03 17:45.\n\nWhy this is separate from S3-GIT-007: that row is the hang (a write-path/stall defect). This row is detection. Fixing the hang does not stop the next silent multi-day outage - a bounded-retry rule plus an alert after N consecutive failures does. N=3 would have surfaced this within ~7 hours instead of 3 days.\n\nNote: several other S3-adjacent jobs are also enabled with last_status=error and are not being watched in any central place (e.g. 'Dreaming - Overnight Memory Synthesis', 'Scan DuckBrain memory and recent chat sessions'), so a shared consecutive-failure rule is worth more than a one-off patch to this script.", "created_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "created_by": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "3d306cecf4bf446442cd50a02f83675d5fc7d289118e0c86c0e90f35bb62a35a"}, "worker_status": "foreman-direct (ops pick, no worker)", "completed_at": "2026-10-06T15:05:00-05:00", "commit_hash": "LANDING", "files_changed": "ops/duckbrain-s3-alert.sh (new), ~/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-alert.sh (installed)", "guard_result": "net rehearsal ALL-PASS (6 cases) + live-fire delivery proof", "ci_result": "not-applicable (ops script; no CI surface)", "foreman_note": "no_agent cron 0633c41279d6 */15min, threshold 4 consecutive FAILs, deliver telegram:-1003310984808:2065. Proven: dirty fire failed|delivered + cron_incidents alerted; clean fire completed|suppressed. Script installed + mirrored to ops/."}
+{"id": "S3-ALERT-001", "title": "P2 — the S3 backup can fail for DAYS with no alert: nothing watches for consecutive failures", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "S3-GIT-007's outage was invisible for 3+ days. The backup itself is a silent watchdog and there is no layer above it that notices repeated failure.\n\nEvidence (live, 2026-10-03):\n  * cron job 1229f45f3ea4 'DuckBrain S3 unified backup (15min)', schedule */15, enabled=true, last_status=error, last_error='Script timed out after 7200s: /home/kara/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-unified.sh'.\n  * The job is no_agent (script-only), so a failure produces no agent turn and no message. Nothing surfaced the outage; it was found only because the owner happened to ask about the S3 tier.\n  * Last SUCCESSFUL run 2026-09-30T12:46:44Z; cadence markers s3-git-pass.last and s3-git-push.last both frozen at that instant; 354 successful runs before it.\n  * The wrapper has no failure-escalation path: grep -i 'consecutive|alert|notify' against ~/.hermes/scripts/duckbrain-s3-unified.sh returns nothing.\n  * Observed failure loop: native-sync log shows 'STALE LOCK cleared pid=X age=~7500-8400s' every ~2h15m with a 'FAIL rc=143' (the 7200s SIGTERM) between, from 10-02 21:15 through 10-03 17:45.\n\nWhy this is separate from S3-GIT-007: that row is the hang (a write-path/stall defect). This row is detection. Fixing the hang does not stop the next silent multi-day outage - a bounded-retry rule plus an alert after N consecutive failures does. N=3 would have surfaced this within ~7 hours instead of 3 days.\n\nNote: several other S3-adjacent jobs are also enabled with last_status=error and are not being watched in any central place (e.g. 'Dreaming - Overnight Memory Synthesis', 'Scan DuckBrain memory and recent chat sessions'), so a shared consecutive-failure rule is worth more than a one-off patch to this script.", "created_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.