 {"id": "OPS-SCHED-UNTRACKED-001", "title": "P2 \u2014 production duckbrain ops scripts are SCHEDULED but exist only in ~/.hermes/scripts (no version control)", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "complexity": 3, "reasoning": "Extends the S3-GIT-007 root cause beyond scripts/s3. That row's fix committed the three wrapper components and added scripts/s3/test-component-completeness.sh, which guards that directory only. The same defect - production runs a script the repo cannot see - is live for at least 9 other duckbrain ops scripts, and 4 of them are demonstrably scheduled.\n\nConfirmed SCHEDULED but UNTRACKED (not in the repo at all):\n  * duckbrain-leg-watchdog.sh        <- cron job 3541777ce883 'DuckBrain agent-leg watchdog' daily 08:00, enabled, ok\n  * reports-s3-push.sh               <- cron job 2ee8eaa9612c 'reports-s3-git-push' daily 03:50, enabled, ok\n  * duckbrain-ns-gc.sh               <- system crontab: '10 4 * * *' (only entry in crontab -l)\n  * duckbrain-ns-repack.sh           <- systemd --user timer duckbrain-ns-repack.timer, weekly (last ran 2026-09-27 04:29)\n\nAlso untracked, currently unscheduled or indirect: dagger-duckbrain-sync-loop.sh, duckbrain-mirror-sync.sh, duckbrain-session-summary.sh, pm-duckbrain-log-20260827.sh, s3-backup.sh.\n\nFour independent scheduling surfaces are in play: hermes cron, system crontab, systemd --user timers, and (for the S3 wrapper) whatever invokes it. audit of systemd --user timers also shows duckbrain-http-health, duckbrain-http-recover and stray-duckbrain-reaper units - additional ops that are not reviewable from the repo.\n\nConsequence, demonstrated: duckbrain-ns-repack.sh (6,142 B, weekly, last ran 2026-09-27) can rewrite namespace storage on a schedule, and no reviewer can read, diff, test or roll it back. Same for the leg watchdog and the reports S3 push, both of which run successfully today but silently drift from the repo.\n\nSuggested fix: extend the completeness invariant past scripts/s3 - either (a) a scripts/ops/ directory holding every scheduled duckbrain script, with a guard that greps each scheduler surface (crontab, systemd --user, hermes cron jobs db) and fails when a scheduled script has no tracked counterpart, or (b) at minimum an inventory file listing every scheduled script, its schedule and its owner, with CI failing on drift.", "created_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "created_by": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "6fe3d61dcb680d4a68ae77f19d3ac9e7822835bea43cec601f99a1b9463c3933"}}
 {"id": "GATE-S3-AMBIG-001", "title": "P2 — the e2e gate cannot tell 'S3 unreachable' from 'write path broken': both legs fail as s3=None with no reason", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "ops/e2e-gate.py LEG 5 is the only leg that proves the S3 write path. When the object store is unreachable or unconfigured, the leg does not say so - it fails with `s3=None`, which is indistinguishable from a genuine break in the write path. A gate whose failure cannot be interpreted is worse than no leg, because it trains the reader to ignore it.\n\nEvidence:\n  * Live run on bunker agent db-ver-1003 (dedi-2), fresh clone of main@095eeb5: VERDICT 14/16, the only two failures being 'L5 S3 rows == disk rows -- s3=None disk=93' and 'L5 S3 contains zero junk -- s3 junk=None'. Disk, audit and API all agreed at 93 in the same run, so three of four channels were healthy and the fourth was simply not connected.\n  * No reachability preflight exists: grep -nE 's3 status|s3 config|reachable|preflight|head_bucket' ops/e2e-gate.py returns nothing before the leg runs.\n  * The failure is swallowed by bare handlers: lines 305 and 313 are `except Exception:` -> `s3n = None` / `s3junk = None`. The exception text (which would have said 'connection refused' or 'no credentials') is discarded.\n  * Contributing: the object path at line 299 hardcodes the bucket ('s3://duckbrain/{prefix}/...') while the SETUP block also hardcoded the endpoint and AWS_PROFILE. Parameterizing endpoint/profile (branch ops/gate-s3-endpoint) makes the gate runnable off-origin but does not fix this ambiguity - the leg still reports None.\n\nSuggested fix: before LEG 5, probe S3 (s3 status / a HEAD on the bucket). If unreachable, record the two S3 legs as SKIPPED-with-reason ('S3 not configured/unreachable: <error>') rather than FAILED, or keep them FAILED but include the captured exception text in the detail. Either way a reader must be able to tell 'not connected' from 'connected and wrong'.", "created_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-04T17:35:00Z", "created_by": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "0feebda244c4719313212df512f4eb0eb55380a5a513a97901b6f37b22f4c4df"}, "commit_hash": "079cd1f", "foreman_note": "Tick duckbrain-foreman-2026-10-04-17-13-42: fixed by the same commit as GATE-PARTITION-001 (1960fee on ops/gate-s3-endpoint, merged --no-ff as 079cd1f): _count_from() returns (None, why) with the first Error: line instead of a bare None, LEG 5 prints '! LEG 5 could not read the object store: <reason>' when the store is unreachable. Verified live: gate run 16/16 ALL GREEN with S3 legs passing against the real endpoint; the ambiguity path was code-reviewed line-by-line (count-parse failure now carries the underlying error text). ch:trace row=GATE-S3-AMBIG-001 spec=none:none wave=none:none test=ops/e2e-gate.py::LEG5 doc=none:none prompt=none:none evidence=e2e-gate-run-16of16-ALL-GREEN-2026-10-04 witness=live:e2e-gate-port-10412 verdict=none:ops-run-no-gitreins-task commit=079cd1f memory=none"}
 {"id": "LOCKFILE-DRIFT-001", "title": "P3 \u2014 four non-pnpm lockfiles are tracked in a pnpm-only repo; one is still maintained, three are ~6 months stale", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "complexity": 2, "reasoning": "The repo declares one package manager and CI enforces it, but ships lockfiles for three. A newcomer cannot tell which is authoritative, and a dependency bump can silently update the wrong one.\n\nEvidence:\n  * package.json: \"packageManager\": \"pnpm@12.4.2\". CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml:31-35) asserts `test \"$(pnpm --version)\" = \"12.4.2\"` then `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. release.yml:30 uses `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. No workflow anywhere runs npm ci, bun or yarn (grep over .github/workflows/* returns only pnpm).\n  * Tracked lockfiles: pnpm-lock.yaml (authoritative), package-lock.json, bun.lock, docs/package-lock.json, packages/ui/package-lock.json.\n  * Age split: pnpm-lock.yaml and package-lock.json were both touched by 3051241 (chore(deps): DEPS-005, 2026-10-03) - so the non-authoritative root npm lockfile is still being maintained by dependency bumps even though no build path reads it. bun.lock last changed cfc85aa (2026-03-31), docs/package-lock.json 01613f8 (2026-04-04), packages/ui/package-lock.json d8aa575 (2026-04-02): all ~6 months stale.\n  * Strictly a drift case: packages/ui/package.json exists and packages/ui is matched by the pnpm workspace glob (pnpm-workspace.yaml packages: [\"packages/*\"]), yet it carries its own npm package-lock.json. Two lockfiles describe the same package set under two resolvers.\n\nPrior related rows CI-002 and CI-003 (both complete) fixed lockfile DESYNC - a lockfile that did not match package.json. This row is different: it is stale/redundant lockfiles for package managers the project does not use, plus continued maintenance of one of them.\n\nSuggested fix: decide authority (pnpm, per packageManager) and delete the unused lockfiles, or document explicitly why each is kept. If npm support is intentional, CI needs an npm ci leg, otherwise the file will drift again.", "created_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T18:13:14.740813+00:00", "created_by": null, "detail": {"fingerprint": "a414597fb68098fdc83781fff5f3f7d689ab0dd76030de6e59e82dd1403b735e"}}
